  "OverallSummary": "This case involves Laura Casey's willful violation of Reg BI's Care Obligation and unauthorized discretionary trading. Between March and July 2022, Casey conducted short-term securities transactions in four retail customers' brokerage accounts when they also maintained advisory accounts at Morgan Stanley. The Care Obligation violation stemmed from Casey's failure to consider the comparative costs of transactions between account types. Specifically, Casey: (1) purchased ETFs and other products in brokerage accounts that required upfront sales charges that would not have been charged in advisory accounts; (2) sold these securities within days of purchase, generating additional sales charges; and (3) in some cases, used the proceeds to make further purchases with additional sales charges. Casey had no reasonable basis to believe these transactions were in the customers' best interests given their intended short holding periods and the associated costs. The violations would have resulted in $37,757.54 in unnecessary sales charges across the four customers, but Morgan Stanley identified Casey's misconduct and reversed the transactions, preventing actual customer losses. Additionally, Casey executed at least 46 trades in at least seven customers' accounts without prior written authorization, violating FINRA Rules 3260(b) and 2010. For her misconduct, Casey received a seven-month suspension and a $7,500 fine."
